MCP is designed for agent-to-tool and agent-to-data connections, while A2A focuses on agent discovery and agent-to-agent communication. AG-UI standardises streaming events such as messages, tool calls, results, and run completion, which fits HyperCode’s real-time dashboard very well.

1. Make MCP the Tool Layer
HyperCode should expose its capabilities through a controlled MCP gateway rather than allowing every agent to connect directly to everything.

Recommended MCP servers
hypercode-files: read and write approved project files.

hypercode-github: branches, issues, pull requests, Actions, releases.

hypercode-docker: inspect and manage approved containers.

hypercode-memory: retrieve and store project knowledge.

hypercode-tests: run tests, linting, builds, and type checks.

hypercode-observability: health checks, logs, traces, and metrics.

hypercode-creative: image, music, video, and 3D-printing workflows.

hypercode-learning: convert technical tasks into accessible lessons.

Use least privilege for every tool. For example, a planning agent may read files but must not push code, while a release agent may publish only after tests and human approval.

OWASP’s MCP guidance identifies token exposure, privilege escalation, tool poisoning, dependency tampering, command injection, prompt injection, weak authorisation, poor telemetry, shadow servers, and context over-sharing as major risks.

