Approval Pattern
Do not expose an approval action as a fake UI-only button. It needs a second authenticated API call to the policy layer.

text
AG-UI stream
  └── hypercode.approval.required
          │
          ▼
Dashboard displays approval card
          │
          ▼
POST /api/approvals/{approval_id}
          │
          ├── approve_once
          ├── deny
          └── ask_for_explanation
                  │
                  ▼
Policy service records immutable decision
                  │
                  ▼
Orchestrator resumes or terminates the run
The run should enter a clear waiting_approval status and include a timeout. A denied approval should emit an understandable policy event and finish safely, not leave a zombie agent run.

Production Rules
One public AG-UI gateway, not a separate browser connection to every container.

Authenticate before opening the stream; bind runId, user identity, workspace, and permissions.

Authorise every tool server-side; frontend events are display data, never authority.

Persist before emitting important events; the dashboard should recover after a refresh.

Use runId, threadId, traceId, and parentRunId for every event.

Redact data before streaming, especially secret-containing tool arguments and logs.

Set a heartbeat roughly every 15–30 seconds to prevent reverse proxies from silently dropping long streams.

Make the client reconnectable; replay missed events from GET /api/runs/{run_id}/events?after=sequence.

Cap event and log sizes; store giant logs in object storage and stream only summaries plus an authorised link.

Never let AG-UI bypass policy checks for Docker, GitHub pushes, deployment, secrets, or shell commands.

OWASP identifies risks around token exposure, privilege escalation, tool poisoning, command injection, weak authorisation, inadequate telemetry, and excessive context sharing in MCP-enabled systems; the same controls matter at your AG-UI-to-tool boundary.

